TRIAL_END {"condition": "fuzzy", "stop_reason": "agent_finished", "agent_seconds": 479.55994134396315, "real_wall_seconds": 490.776000325568, "gpu_queue_wait_seconds": 11.141089411452413, "api_calls": 16, "tool_actions": 20, "generated_tokens": 27680, "prompt_tokens": 372610, "total_tokens": 400290, "reported_cost_usd": 0.0159372528, "failed_requests_usage_unknown": 0, "final_source_sha256": "1030456123091f5e38ae1d5dcaa4ca9318e414fd0ccae4712d4ac62ff4520cc2", "final_answer": "I'm out of budget for further edits. The last saved source was already validated by the final benchmark run (no edits since). Here is my report.\n\n## Final report\n\n**Result:** All three public workloads pass correctness (loss, input-grad, weight-grad relative errors well within tolerance) with substantial speedups vs. the frozen baseline:\n\n| Family | Baseline (µs) | Final (µs) | Speedup |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| small | 1594.8 | 981.4 | **1.63×** |\n| main | 6525.6 | 4411.5 | **1.48×** |\n| wide_hidden | 6029.7 | 4286.6 | **1.41×** |\n\nGeometric-mean speedup ≈ **1.50×**.\n\n### What I changed (only `fused_linear_cross_entropy.py`)\n\n1. **Fused weight-gradient accumulation.** The old path did `grad_weight += torch.mm(grad_logits.T, input).float()` into a bf16 accumulator — materializing a bf16 mm output *and* a full fp32 `V×H` upcast purely to add it straight back into a bf16 buffer (a rounding no-op). Replaced with a single `torch.addmm(grad_weight, grad_logits.T, input, out=grad_weight)` that accumulates in fp32 inside cuBLAS and rounds once. This was the single largest win.\n\n2. **Collapsed the chunk loop to one chunk** (`_CHUNK_MEM_CONST` 16→32). At the public shapes this makes `num_chunks == 1` everywhere, removing redundant chunk boundaries/accumulation and improving both GEMM shapes and the numerics of the input gradient (main's input-grad rel. error dropped from 2.8e-3 to 1.7e-5).\n\n3. **Skipped the wasted weight-gradient memset.** With one chunk the buffer is fully overwritten, so it is now allocated with `empty_like` and filled via `torch.mm(..., out=grad_weight)` instead of `zeros_like` + accumulate — eliminating a full `V×H` zero-fill that the next matmul immediately destroyed.\n\n4. **Removed a hidden per-call host sync/allocation in backward.** `torch.equal(grad_output, torch.tensor(1.0, device=...))` allocated a tensor and ran a GPU reduction + sync every call; replaced with `grad_output.numel() != 1 or grad_output.item() != 1.0`.\n\n### Notes\nThe dominant cost is the three GEMMs (logits, input-grad, weight-grad), which are already near cuBLAS peak (~120 TFLOP/s of the A6000's ~155 dense bf16), so the wins above came from removing the surrounding bandwidth waste (fp32 upcast, memset) and fixed per-call overhead rather than from the matmuls themselves. Remaining headroom is mostly the CE kernel's unavoidable 2-read/1-write pass over the logits, which I did not get to restructure within budget.\n\nThe last saved source is the validated, correct version.", "namespace": "20260928-093511-r1-fuzzy", "budget_notices": 16, "api_seconds": 410.51785787101835, "tool_seconds": 79.1165889473632, "benchmark_seconds": 78.6794571839273, "benchmark_active_seconds": 67.53836777247488}
